WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.740
In Counter-Strike: Global Offensive, the Major is everything.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:13.420
There are other big tournaments, with their own prestige and their own history.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.800
But Majors are in a class of their own.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.400
The prize pool and the bragging rights stand apart.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:26.400
And, until January, North America had yet to win a single one.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.940
"Like this is the joke we had all weekend long where it's like

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:35.020
a Kazakhstani team won the Major. Where you at now NA."

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:40.280
"What up now NA WOO!"

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:49.620
"Kazakhstan you know, ehh ehh. North America. What is going on here."

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:57.640
At the 11 prior official Valve Majors, only six NA teams had made it out of the main group stage.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:03.760
Only one had reached the grand final — Team Liquid, whose roster was majority American

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.700
but anchored by Ukrainian superstar Oleksandr "s1mple" Kostyliev.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:14.720
That team made an incredible run at Cologne in 2016, but it ended when they fell to Brazilian

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.920
powerhouse SK Gaming.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:24.020
The constant NA heartbreak had to have a source, but it was difficult to pinpoint what exactly what that might be

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.120
"And so, the talent is there in the United States.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.200
what's always been the hold up it seems is the buyouts, how expensive it is, how expensive it would be.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:39.480
and so or you just kind of have to cheat a little bit and go full liquid and get one of the best European players

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:42.740
out there and maybe use him as a standin."

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:47.500
Given North America’s history at the Majors, fans were less than hopeful that the streak

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.840
would be broken in Boston.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.260
But the region’s the best bet was Cloud9.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:57.500
The team had gone through changes in August 2017, with n0thing and Shroud leaving the

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.780
roster, which seemed to highlight internal strife.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:05.640
"Kind of couple weeks before it ended, I kind of felt there was definitely internal conflict in the team.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:09.720
Things like that are bound to happen you have different age levels, different maturity levels.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.620
Stuff like that is just bound to come up. No point into getting team stuff I believe that stays in the team."

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.500
"There is always kind of a barrier right, and this is the unwritten rule

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:21.800
when it comes to players is whatever is happening in the team, you don't talk about it with anyone. It's like mob

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:26.740
families. Whatever's happening in the family, that's family business and you are not hearing about it.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.720
right you're not talking about that to anybody else."

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.060
"It's not much to talk about really, two weeks ago or something I messaged Jack and I was like "Hey."

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:39.820
Jack's the owner of C9 by the way I was like "Hey. I kind of want to step down. I would love to be your guys'

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.420
backup, I wanna see where full time stream takes me."

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:47.020
And he was super supportive he was like "Let's do it. Let's make it happen." Let the record state.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.820
That I asked to see myself out."

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:54.900
Replacing them were Tarik and RUSH, who had recently completed a stint with OpTic Gaming

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.440
that included several high-profile victories.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:04.200
"NA it's kinda of dire I don't really have all that much faith in this new Cloud9

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.920
because it seems like they're still playing hot potato with the ingame leader role."

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:11.520
The move may not have inspired confidence initially, but public opinion eventually began

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.080
to turn.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:17.400
And the team started putting up promising results at the perfect time: the months preceding

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:21.560
the Boston Major, set for January 2018.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.160
The strength of those results even led several analysts to rank them among the Major’s

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.440
Top 5 teams.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:32.240
Because the roster still included three players who had helped the team achieve a 9th-11th

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.370
finish at the previous major in Krakow, they were automatically qualified for the Boston

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:39.960
Major’s “New Challengers” stage.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:46.320
It used a Swiss format, and C9 quickly put up wins against Envy, Sprout and Mouseports

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.020
to earn their spot in the New Legends stage.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.560
Fans held their breath.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:55.980
Though the team had another swiss group stage to make it through, it was an encouraging

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.860
performance so far.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:01.920
But things immediately got worse.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:38.120
(Casting)

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:43.680
Down 0-2, Cloud9 looked like they would continue NA’s Major legacy by bombing out.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:50.220
"Yeah that first half it seems some of the problems I left them with when I was off the team, is still there.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:55.540
I don't know, even watching the last round there. I stood behind the players as I got ready to come on the desk

00:04:55.540 --> 00:05:00.460
and it just seemed like they were kind of upset, they're probably gonna do an interview and say they felt

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.520
like they should have won because some of the rounds. Like the very last round they lost Tarik

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.900
there's a miscom, Rush was supposed to peek A hall or something and Tarik said wait I thought

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:11.600
thought you were holding the push. So I don't know if they started getting in their own heads a little bit."

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:17.280
But for some members of C9, like Stewie2k, being 0-2 changed something.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.020
"Uhm I think after the Space Soldiers game it was a big wake up call for me. Like I said

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:26.860
I think it kind of lit the fuel for me. That's where the fire started happening. I DMd all night,

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:31.620
I would play until - I think I only got a few hours of sleep the next day. But that's something I'm willing to sacrifice

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:36.900
if it makes me play better. And from there I just kind of didn't look back and played on my own game."

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:43.560
"Yeah yesterday, losing to G2 fine, we lost to G2 whatever. Losing to Space Soldiers was a big hit

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:49.520
cause once you got 0-2 you know like sh*t hit the fan and you know like now it's elimination matches only."

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:54.700
With their tournament life now on the line, they first played against Virtus.pro, another

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:57.249
0-2 team, thanks to the Swiss format.

00:05:57.249 --> 00:06:02.900
C9 put up a convincing 16-7 effort on Mirage and lived to fight another day.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:17.640
(Casting)

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.480
Next, Astralis.

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:24.029
A team that had won the previous ELEAGUE Major in Atlanta, but struggled to find their form

00:06:24.029 --> 00:06:27.200
as an injury to Dev1ce affected their fortunes.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.680
But even with Dev1ce back, C9 overcame the Danish roster.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:44.640
(Casting)

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:52.080
Now 2-2, there was only a single obstacle between North America and the bracket stage:

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:53.259
Vega Squadron.

00:06:53.259 --> 00:06:58.219
A surprise in their own right, Vega was a CIS team that had been together for a long

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:01.960
time but failed to show up against Tier 1 teams.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:04.960
One of the cinderella runs had to end.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:28.780
(Casting)

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.920
Now in the bracket and on the big stage, Cloud9 were playing on home soil.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:46.200
They would need to win a best-of-three against G2 Esports to move on.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:50.680
And despite their earlier loss to the French powerhouse, they didn’t just win

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:52.800
they won it convincingly.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:59.560
(Casting)

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:04.400
But SK Gaming would be their opponents in the semifinals, and that team had been on

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:09.040
a tear of their own before the tournament albeit with boltz in place of felps.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:30.000
(Casting)

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:35.120
After a dominant game one performance by Cloud9, SK struck back.

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:55.680
(Casting)

00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:02.280
But a standout performance from Tarik helped boost his team, and Cloud9 made it through to the finals.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:08.880
(Casting)

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:13.240
Their last opponent: the superteam roster of FaZe Clan.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:18.079
The team that seemed all but destined to lift the trophy in Boston.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:24.240
While North America was still looking for their first Major win, FaZe’s Olofmeister already boasted two.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:30.120
"Of course it will be very fun to win three and to join the likes of Pronax, JW, and Flusha."

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:35.080
But that squad still had players hungry for their first, including veteran AWPer GuardiaN

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:37.040
and superstar young gun NiKo.

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:43.440
On home soil, with the bewildered but exhilarated crowd behind them, Cloud9 went to work.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:10:51.120
(Casting)

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:54.160
In a close map, FaZe claimed victory on Mirage.

00:10:54.520 --> 00:11:00.940
NA was one map away from elimination, from missing another Major title, after losing

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:02.260
their own map pick.

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:07.520
But Cloud9 didn’t back down, taking FaZe’s own map of Overpass away from them.

00:11:08.480 --> 00:12:18.120
(Casting)

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:23.600
And map 3 — Inferno — had never seen a contest between these two teams.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:29.600
The resulting game… well, it’s unlikely that we’ll ever see another one like it.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:13:53.920
(Casting)

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:59.080
Down 14-9, Cloud9’s hopes looked dim, but they would not be stopped.

00:13:59.080 --> 00:15:22.880
(Casting)

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:28.320
Overtime. In a Major. Clinched by an outnumbered Stewie2K.

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:29.740
Madness.

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:33.720
C9 had won 6 of 7 rounds to force their comeback.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:38.280
And in the first overtime, it looked like that momentum was clearly with them.

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:41.840
But GuardiaN desperately forced a second OT.

00:15:41.920 --> 00:16:05.760
(Casting)

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:09.360
And that OT began with a last second defuse.

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:30.160
(Casting)

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:34.000
But C9 pulled ahead. They were on match point.

00:16:34.320 --> 00:17:34.080
(Casting)

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:42.200
"This has to be one of the most incredible story lines we've ever had. A North American team.

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:44.640
in North America with a Major win.

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:51.360
As the team lifted the trophy and the typically quiet Skadoodle offered up the post-game interview,

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:53.160
"I'm not crying, you're crying, stop."

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:55.560
it was nearly too surreal.

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:59.080
NA had a Major win.

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:05.200
But it wasn’t to last.

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:09.840
Whether it was the issues that n0thing spoke of or simply a wealth of new opportunities,

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:15.640
the Cloud9 roster that won in Boston didn’t make it to the London Major intact.

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:21.440
After losing in the Grand Finals of cs_summit_2, Stewie2K left to play with Brazilian squad

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:22.520
SK Gaming.

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:28.320
And after being such a huge part of the team’s run at Boston, Tarik left the team in July

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:31.520
to join Stewie on the roster now called MiBR.

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:34.840
Although they were unable able to defend their Major title.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:37.840
C9 drew first blood for the region.

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:43.070
They showed us what is likely the greatest Major grand final series so far.

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:47.200
They did it against some of the strongest teams in CS.

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:49.920
And fans will never forget.

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:52.320
"They weren't the favourites, they were the underdogs."

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:56.880
"This has to be one of the most incredible story lines we've ever had."

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:03.600
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

